es%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6002.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>Main Deity: In the basement (cellar) of the temple of Shri Shitalnath Bhagwan is an image of Bhagwan Shri Lodhan Parshwanath in high-black colored stone, in a half-padmasana posture, 41 inches tall and 33 inches wide. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 7 hoods of a serpent (naga).</p><p>Location: This is in Dabhoi city, in Shamalaji ni Sheri.</p><p>History: The history of this magnificent and artistic image is very interesting. Sagardatta Shreshthi used to travel from village to village for trade, loading goods onto his bullocks (pothi). Once, when he came to the city of Darbhavati, the Chaturmas (four-month monsoon retreat) began and he stayed on there. He had taken a vow to eat only after worshipping the Tirthankara Paramatma. So, on his mother&#x27;s advice, he obtained an attractive image of Lord Parshwanath made of sand and installed it in the temple in his home. He worshipped this image daily with great faith and devotion. When the Chaturmas ended, he had to return to his hometown, so he consigned the image to a well in the center of the city. A few years later, Sagardatta again came to Darbhavati for trade. At midnight he had a dream in which a divine voice told him to retrieve the image of Lord Parshwanath from the well where he had placed it. In the morning, he asked members of the Jain community to help him bring the image out of the well. The image was successfully drawn out of the well in a sieve tied with thread by a young unmarried girl (kunwari kanya). The image was placed in a bullock cart, and without anyone guiding it, the cart began to move forward. It was decided that a temple would be built wherever the cart stopped. A temple was built at the spot where the bullock cart came to rest, and the image was installed there. The image, made of sand, had remained in deep water for a long time, but not a single grain of sand fell away, and the image had become as hard as iron -- hence this image came to be known as &quot;Lodhan Parshwanath.&quot; This image is more than 600 years old. This temple was last rebuilt in Vikram era 1990. As the image of Lord Parshwanath is made of sand (velu), it is also known as &quot;Shri Velu Parshwanath.&quot; Mahopadhyay Shri Yashovijayji passed away (attained kaldharma) in this very city in Vikram Samvat 1743. This is a very miraculous image, and miracles continue to occur here. A woman who performed the chaityavandan (ritual salutation) of this powerful image of Bhagwan Shri Lodhan Parshwanath with great faith and devotion was blessed with a vision of Dharanendra Dev (Nagdev).</p><p>Other Temples: Nearby is the ancient temple of Shamla Parshwanath. There are 3 other temples here. As mentioned earlier, the 8th Parshwanath in this account, Shri Pragatprabhavi Parshwanath, is also in Dabhoi, at Shrimali Vaga, so here one gets the benefit of the darshan and worship of two of the 108 Parshwanaths.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: Although this image is made of sand, it appears to be made of iron. Its artistry is wondrous and captivates the eye.</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station is Dabhoi, 2 kilometers away. From Dabhoi railway station, Vadodara is 32 kilometers away. From Vadodara, the Chhani pilgrimage site (108th, Shri Vimal Parshwanath) is 8 km away. From Manilaxmi Tirth, Chhani is 57 km away. Buses, taxis and auto-rickshaws are available here. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available here. Since ancient times, Dabhoi has been a great center for Jains. There are many temples, upashrayas, ayambil halls and knowledge repositories (gyan bhandars) here. The Hera Fort-Bhagol is a place of historical importance here.</p><p>Scripture References: Shri Lodhan Parshwanath is mentioned in &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Bhatev Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri 108 Naam Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaitya Paripati,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Nammala,&quot; &quot;135 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;108 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; and others. There is an image of Lodhan Parshwanath at the Shri Kalikund Parshwanath temple in Santacruz, Mumbai.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: Who appeared from a well by a raw cotton thread, the Lord made of sand who became like iron, who is enshrined in padmasana, reigning in Darbhavati -- &quot;I bow with devotion to Shri Lodhan Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Lodhan Parshwanathay Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Seth Shri Devchandji Dharamchandji Pedhi, Shri Lodhan Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Shrimali Vaga, Shamalaji ni Sheri (Darbhavati), Mu. Po.: Dabhoi-391110, District: Vadodara, State of Gujarat, India. Phone: 02663-2258150, Mobile: 09426375123.</p><p>The Second and Third Births of Bhagwan Parshwanath: The Second Birth as Yuthapati Gaja -- Having ended his life in a state of distressed contemplation (artadhyan), the soul of Marubhuti was reborn as Yuthapati Gajaraja, the lord of elephants, among the herds of the Vindhyachal mountain range. Kamath&#x27;s wife Varuna, after death, was reborn as Yuthapati Gajaraja&#x27;s beloved she-elephant. On the other side, when the king learned that Kamath had killed his own brother Marubhuti, he became detached from this selfish world, and taking monastic initiation, embraced restraint (samyam) and went to the Vindhyachal mountains to practice supreme austerities. One day, when the king (now a monk) stood in meditation in these Vindhyachal mountains, the soul of Marubhuti, now the elephant-king, came there, and seeing the monk in meditation, Gajaraja was overcome with jatismaran gyan (the knowledge of past lives). Recalling his previous birth through this jatismaran, the elephant-king accepted the Shravaka Dharma (lay follower&#x27;s vows) before the monk. Once a terrible fire broke out in the forests of the Vindhyachal, and as the whole forest began to burn, the elephant-king entered the waters of a lake to save his life and stood there. At that time, the soul of Kamath, who had died and been reborn as a snake of the Kurkat species, was passing overhead through the sky, and his gaze fell upon the elephant-king standing in the lake. The moment his eyes fell on the elephant-king, the enmity harbored by Kamath&#x27;s soul, now the Kurkat serpent, was awakened, and in anger he bit the elephant on the head (forehead). The venom from the bite spread through the elephant-king&#x27;s entire body, but the elephant-king bore the pain with equanimity and completed his lifespan in that same state of equanimity (the Second Birth). The Third Birth as the God Sahasrar -- Having taken the vow to observe the Shravaka Dharma and having completed his lifespan while remaining in a state of equanimity, the soul of Marubhuti was reborn as a god named Sahasrar in the eighth heaven (devalok) (the Third Birth). This shrine is known as the 8th -- Shri Lodhan Parshwanath Prabhu Temple, Dabhoi (Darbhavati).</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-010\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Pragatprabhavi Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-012\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Vimal Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl